WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:16.740
What if I told you that the Iraq War was&nbsp;
perhaps the United States’ greatest act of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.180
strategic stupidity in the last 30 years. At least&nbsp;
taking into account what is called realpolitik.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.380
Think about it for a moment: what are the main&nbsp;
threats to Washington's interests? China, Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.980
North Korea and Iran. Remember that Iran is a&nbsp;
regional power in the Middle East. They have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.060
almost 90 million inhabitants and tons of natural&nbsp;
resources, with the second largest natural gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.980
reserves in the world and the fourth largest oil&nbsp;
reserves. In short, Iran occupies a key strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.240
position in the Middle East. What’s more, Tehran&nbsp;
is on the verge of developing its own atomic bomb.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.660
Well, having to deal with such an adversary in&nbsp;
this corner of the planet, it turns out that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.800
greatest wall of containment against Iranian&nbsp;
expansionism was none other than Saddam Hussein.
 &nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:57.840
Saddam's Iraq was a savage dictatorship,&nbsp;
yes, but absolutely anti-Iranian. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.140
then the United States arrived 20 years ago,&nbsp;
and could not think of anything better to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.820
than to deliver Iraq on a silver platter to Iran.
 
Here you go, Khamenei, have at it. You may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.360
think that I’m exaggerating. But 20 years&nbsp;
later, I assure you that this is the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.780
things went down. In fact, I'm sure many of&nbsp;
you remember that when the Iraq war started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.860
memes like this one were passed around:
Well, strange as it may seem, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.240
exactly what has happened. Because today there&nbsp;
is a democracy in Iraq. So, who has seized power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.200
in the Iraqi democracy? Well, none other than&nbsp;
the Shiite leaders closest to Iran. What’s more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.440
do you remember Qasem Soleimani, the&nbsp;
commander of the Quds Force? Well, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.400
Iranian general was at ease in Iraq, organizing&nbsp;
the Shiite militias operating in the country.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.120
In fact, the US succeeded in taking down&nbsp;
General Soleimani in Baghdad. And just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.600
show you how things are going, the Iraqi&nbsp;
capital has added a new place of interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.340
to its tourist guides. Check it out:
 
(The wreckage from the U.S. drone strike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.740
that killed Qasem Soleimani &amp; Abu Mahdi&nbsp;
al-Mohandes in Iraq last year is now a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.180
memorial site on Baghdad's airport road)
 
You have to admit that even Turkmenistan has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.720
more taste when it comes to making monuments.&nbsp;
But anyway, back to the point, the bottom line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.980
is that Iran represents a growing threat as it&nbsp;
gets closer and closer to having the atomic bomb.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.440
And now it is expanding its influence over Iraq,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.520
the world's fourth largest oil exporter and&nbsp;
the second largest within OPEC. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.240
VisualPolitik viewers, the situation could be&nbsp;
about to change in favor of the United States.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.340
So the question is: To what extent might Iranian&nbsp;
influence over Iraq dissipate? What role will this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.420
gentleman you are seeing on the screen right now,&nbsp;
who looks more like advertising for Revenge of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.860
Sith than an Iraqi political leader, play?
 
Today on VisualPolitik we are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.680
answer these questions but first...&nbsp;
let's take a look at some history.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.580
(INTIMATE ENEMIES)

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.780
There are three key ideas that I would&nbsp;
like to remind you of. The first one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.100
which we have already mentioned a lot of times,&nbsp;
is that within the Islamic world there is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.300
eternal Cold War between the two main branches of&nbsp;
Islam: Sunnism and Shiism. The struggle between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.980
these branches of the Muslim religion dates back&nbsp;
to the year 632, when it was decided who should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.480
succeed Muhammad, so on this point don’t expect&nbsp;
any agreement. About 80% of Muslims are Sunni.
 &nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.160
However, the Islamic Republic of Iran is entirely&nbsp;
Shiite territory. To a lesser extent so is Iraq,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.960
where Shiites make up 65% of the population as&nbsp;
opposed to 30% Sunni. And this brings us to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.100
second important idea: Saddam Hussein's Iraq&nbsp;
was a country ruled by a Sunni elite despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.700
the Shiite social majority. For the third&nbsp;
idea it is better to take a look at the map:&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.060
Here you can see that Iraq and Iran are&nbsp;
two countries that besides having very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.180
similar names are also neighbors.&nbsp;
Neighbors, it needs to be said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.320
who did not get along at all well.
 
They were at loggerheads for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.860
ever since Arab socialism took hold in&nbsp;
Iraq and espoused a nationalism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.080
included territorial claims over Iran. In fact,&nbsp;
Saddam Hussein invaded Iran in the early 1980s.
 &nbsp;

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.880
Then Iran launched its counter-offensive on Iraq.&nbsp;
The war lasted eight years and served no purpose;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.020
the borders did not change. Both countries were&nbsp;
ruined while causing approximately one million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:51.120
deaths. Then came the invasion of Kuwait, the Gulf&nbsp;
War and the invasion of Iraq in 2003. It is then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.980
that our man begins to take center stage.
 
He is Muqtada Al-Sadr.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.780
For those of you who do not know him,&nbsp;
Muqtada Al-Sadr is a cleric and son of Grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.560
Ayatollah Mohamed Sadeq al-Sadr. His father&nbsp;
was an important religious authority for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.980
the Shiites of Iraq, condemned to belong to&nbsp;
the most disadvantaged social classes during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.540
the harsh repression exercised by the Sunni&nbsp;
regime of Saddam Hussein. As an ayatollah, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.740
preached on social and economic issues affecting&nbsp;
people's lives, and thus also became a political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:25.440
icon for the Shiites. His popularity led to his&nbsp;
assassination by Saddam's regime in 1999. 	
 &nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.860
The Shiites were the main opposition&nbsp;
to Saddam's regime. After his fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.040
some of their leaders cooperated with&nbsp;
the United States to work together in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.000
the creation of the new Iraq. This was&nbsp;
not the case with Muqtada Al-Sadr, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.260
was radically opposed to the US occupation. Many&nbsp;
Shiites who had already been followers of Grand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.040
Ayatollah Al-Sadr saw his son as a&nbsp;
symbol of resistance to the invader.
 &nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.000
And so Muqtada Al-Sadr became one of the best&nbsp;
known leaders of the Iraqi insurgency, with great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.320
power and influence in the center and south of the&nbsp;
country, as well as in Baghdad, in the capital's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.300
Al-Zaura suburb, known today as Al-Sadr City.
 
Well, to fight against the invading troops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.580
Muqtada Al-Sadr created a militia, the Mahdi&nbsp;
Army. Operational between 2003 and 2008, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.020
militia reinforced Muqtada Al-Sadr's status as&nbsp;
one of the most influential men in the new Iraq.
 &nbsp;

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.680
However, this was not the best way to come to&nbsp;
power. Because as I was telling you before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.520
the United States established a democracy in&nbsp;
Iraq. And of course, in the Iraqi population,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.380
the Shiites are the majority. So the Iraqi&nbsp;
Ayatollah Ali al-Sistani called on the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.340
Shia parties to present a single list for&nbsp;
the first free elections. Muqtada Al-Sadr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.960
and the other Shiite leaders agreed and the&nbsp;
United Iraqi Alliance won the 2005 elections.
 &nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.360
The United Iraqi Alliance quickly betrayed&nbsp;
its name. In 2007 the coalition collapsed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.380
giving rise to a new struggle for Shiite&nbsp;
political leadership. The main beneficiary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:47.460
was Prime Minister Nuri al-Maliki. Formerly&nbsp;
in exile in Iran, from where he had gained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.600
financial support for the Islamist opposition&nbsp;
to Saddam Hussein, Nuri al-Maliki increased his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.500
popularity among Iraqis as prime minister&nbsp;
by improving security inside the country.
 &nbsp;

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:03.600
Muqtada Al-Sadr moved pragmatically in politics.&nbsp;
He granted or withdrew the support of the Sadrist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.200
Movement depending on the influence he could&nbsp;
gain over the various Ministries. His ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.180
has two pillars: Shiite religious thought and&nbsp;
Iraqi nationalism. It has therefore always been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.780
opposed to foreign interference, whether&nbsp;
from the United States or Iran. US troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:21.000
withdrew in December 2011. However, a new&nbsp;
threat began to spread over Iraq in 2014.
 &nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:28.380
Daesh, the Islamic State, has put the new Iraqi&nbsp;
state in check. Followers of an extreme version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:32.520
of Sunni Islam, they extended the domains of&nbsp;
their caliphate as far as Fallujah, just 69&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.960
kilometers or 42 miles from Baghdad. Faced with&nbsp;
the inability of the Iraqi army to confront them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.680
the various Shiite forces formed their own&nbsp;
paramilitary militias. Along with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.100
Muqtada Al-Sadr, who recovered his&nbsp;
former militia – the Mahdi Army. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.360
After three years of war they achieved victory.&nbsp;
Despite this, the Shiite paramilitary militias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:53.220
which are backed by Iran, have not been disbanded.&nbsp;
For his part, Muqtada Al-Sadr embarked on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.820
strategy of opposition to the government which&nbsp;
has paid off: the Sadrist Movement became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.960
largest group in the Iraqi Parliament. How&nbsp;
did he achieve this? We are about to see.
 &nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:12.540
(OPEN SESAME!)
 
Iraqi democracy is so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.680
like Western versions that in just a decade people&nbsp;
were fed up with their politicians. Since 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:21.780
turnout at elections plummeted to 44%. While&nbsp;
his rivals have fallen into disrepute, Muqtada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.860
al-Sadr has managed to keep his faithful base and&nbsp;
increase his voters. This has so far translated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:30.240
into greater influence over Iraqi affairs.
 
In fact, in parliamentary negotiations his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.080
coalition has obtained its share of jobs&nbsp;
in public companies to offer them to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.100
followers. The creation of these clientelistic&nbsp;
networks has been strengthening and expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.660
his social base. Muqtada al-Sadr has thus gone&nbsp;
from being a simple warlord to a master of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:46.620
political arts. Something quite surprising&nbsp;
considering that in the last few years Iraq&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:50.760
is finally giving some good news.
 
(Baghdad's Green Zone reopens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.600
the public after 16 years – ArabNews)
 
The Green Zone was the only secure area within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:58.980
Baghdad itself. It is where the US Embassy is&nbsp;
located, as well as various government buildings.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.420
Throughout all these years it has been an&nbsp;
armored zone because since the US invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:08.280
Iraq has been a real powder keg. The fact that it&nbsp;
is now open to the public again is the best sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.060
that the situation is relatively calm again.
 
And I’m sure there are a few of you thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:16.380
but [host], what is all this discontent about?&nbsp;
Well, the point is that many Iraqis are seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.920
how their rulers are more concerned about&nbsp;
catering to Iran's wishes than working for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.360
national interests. Iraq is an oil-rich country.&nbsp;
However, the population does not see the benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.800
of their own dinosaur juice. This is evident&nbsp;
if we take a look at the Iraqi state budgets.&nbsp;

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:37.020
Iraq's budget amounts to about $90 billion.&nbsp;
Looking at the dark blue you can see how about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:40.680
two-thirds is swallowed up by the salaries&nbsp;
and pensions of public sector workers. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:44.400
we add to that the capital expenditure, the&nbsp;
truth is that a very small part of the budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.060
is left for the investments that are needed&nbsp;
to improve the living standards of Iraqis.
 &nbsp;

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:54.420
Precisely one sector in need of investment&nbsp;
is the energy sector. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.980
Iraq has gas reserves five times larger than&nbsp;
those of Oman. However, its production is four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.480
times smaller. Its production is so low that it&nbsp;
barely covers half of the country's consumption.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:07.020
And the reality is even more scandalous.&nbsp;
It turns out that Iraq is flaring much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.440
of the natural gas coming out of its oil wells&nbsp;
because it does not have the infrastructure to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.180
capture and use that gas. So who is the main&nbsp;
beneficiary of Iraq needing to import gas? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:21.720
bet you didn't see this move coming:  
 
(Iraq owes Iran $11bn for gas imports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:26.460
says Iranian ambassador – The National News)
 
This suits Iran just fine. Because US sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:30.840
prevent it from exporting its natural resources.&nbsp;
But Washington grants exemptions to Iraq because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.280
the country needs Iranian gas to cover a third of&nbsp;
its electricity production. In fact, when there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.880
are problems with Iran's gas supply, blackouts&nbsp;
occur. Those of you who have been watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.600
VisualPolitik for a long time already know that&nbsp;
we often repeat that countries whose energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.040
supply is not secure have a lot of difficulties&nbsp;
getting their economic sectors to prosper.
 &nbsp;

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.700
Everyone understands this. Even the Iraqis,&nbsp;
who are outraged that their rulers serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.820
Iranian interests more than those of their&nbsp;
own State. However, this is something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.180
is collapsing under its own weight. And not&nbsp;
because of a religious issue, far from it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:04.980
As is obvious, Iraqis are wondering what the&nbsp;
government is doing with the record revenues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:10.200
gained in 2022 from the oil sector: $115&nbsp;
billion, to be exact. And not surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:16.620
when they do get the answers, it is even worse.
 
('Heist of the century': how $2.5bn was plundered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.400
from Iraqi state funds – The Guardian)
 
In Iraq, ghost workers are all the rage. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.820
are workers who do not exist, but they collect&nbsp;
their salaries. In other words, the rulers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:28.260
fictitiously fattened the lists of public workers&nbsp;
to pocket their salaries. It is estimated that 10%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:33.300
of public employees are ghost workers. So, yes,&nbsp;
Iraq has evolved a lot. It is getting safer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.720
there is a democracy. But it is a hyper-corrupt&nbsp;
country. In any other place, people would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.400
criticize it and say: hey, look what a scoundrel&nbsp;
this politician is: he's blatantly stealing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:45.060
money!! But in Iraq it's not that simple.
 
The country has oil coming out its ears;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.360
technically its per capita income is&nbsp;
that of a country like Jordan. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:52.080
the standard of living is not equivalent: public&nbsp;
services leave a lot to be desired and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.340
still even power cuts. And you will probably say,&nbsp;
ok, but is this so important? Well, this is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:00.120
brought the Taliban back to power in Afghanistan.&nbsp;
People were fed up with the corruption and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:04.680
State institutions not working. In the case of&nbsp;
Iraq, how could the situation change? Listen up.
 &nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:16.500
(THE ENEMY OF MY ENEMY)
 
Iraqis already erupted against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:21.060
the Iraqi government in large protests during the&nbsp;
fall of 2019. They demanded an end to corruption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.900
and better public services. They succeeded in&nbsp;
getting the prime minister to resign. Even so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:28.800
the protesters were brutally repressed. But&nbsp;
not at the hands of the State security forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.480
rather at the hands of Shiite militias accountable&nbsp;
only to Iran. The same ones who were waiting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:36.960
orders from General Soleimani.
 
In 2021, this repression took its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.400
toll on Iranian-backed political parties, led&nbsp;
by former Prime Minister Nuri al-Maliki. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:45.480
Sadrist Movement announced that it would seek a&nbsp;
government that excluded all pro-Iranian factions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:50.820
A government by and for Iraq. It gained important&nbsp;
allies for this: Masoud Barzani, leader of Iraqi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:55.620
Kurdistan, and Mohammed al-Halbousi, leader of&nbsp;
the Iraqi Sunnis. Al-Halbousi himself made this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.620 --> 00:12:00.180
statement about the negotiations with al-Sadr.
 
(“The time for foreign interference in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:05.100
formation of Iraqi governments is over, and today&nbsp;
the mountains and deserts of Iraq are moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:10.500
towards the honorable Najaf for discussions of a&nbsp;
purely national Iraqi government, neither eastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:14.940
nor western.” – Mohammed al-Halbousi)
 
"Neither east nor west" is al-Sadr's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.340
favorite slogan to describe how&nbsp;
the Iraqi government should be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:25.320
An Iraqi nationalist proclamation, equally opposed&nbsp;
to both Iranian and US interference and leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:29.700
behind Shiite religious mandates to build a new&nbsp;
Iraq hand in hand with Sunnis and Kurds. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:35.280
meantime, however, Iran did not stand idly by.
 
(Iran piles pressure on Iraq's Kurdish and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.740
Sunni leaders to crack Sadr's&nbsp;
alliance – MiddleEastEyes)
 &nbsp;

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.460
Iran has launched missile and drone&nbsp;
attacks against Iraqi Kurdistan. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:45.420
the Iraqi Supreme Court banned oil exports from&nbsp;
this autonomous region, leaving it without its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:49.140
main source of income. As for the Sunnis, the&nbsp;
pressure was exerted by the Shiite militias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:53.100
who act violently and with total impunity in&nbsp;
Sunni-majority areas. In an attempt to force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:58.620
early elections, Muqtada al-Sadr ordered all his&nbsp;
deputies to resign. A move that has backfired.
 &nbsp;

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:05.160
When the representatives of the Sadrist Movement&nbsp;
gone from Parliament, their seats were taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.700
by members of the pro-Iranian parties. These&nbsp;
forces succeeded in forming a new government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:13.500
with Mohamed Shia al Sudani, a former al-Maliki&nbsp;
minister. Supporters of the Sadrist Movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:17.340
held protests in Baghdad that led to violent&nbsp;
clashes with Iranian-backed Shiite militias.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:21.960
Iraq was on the brink of civil war until Muqtada&nbsp;
al-Sadr ordered the withdrawal of his followers.
 &nbsp;

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:29.460
Iranian pressure also reaches the religious&nbsp;
arena. A central doctrine in Shiite Islam is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:33.120
the concept of marja. It means that every&nbsp;
Shiite Muslim must have a spiritual leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:37.200
whose edicts are unquestionably followed.&nbsp;
Muqtada's father, Ayatollah Mohammed al-Sadr,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:41.760
did have the qualifications to be a spiritual&nbsp;
leader. But Muqtada does not. So when al-Sadr's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:44.940
father was assassinated, the followers of the&nbsp;
Sadrist Movement were forced to follow another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:49.080
cleric in religious matters and turned to&nbsp;
Ayatollah al-Haeri. It is a lifetime job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:54.300
but... it seems that that no longer applies. 
 
(Al-Haeri retires from working as a religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:58.620
authority and advises the believers to follow&nbsp;
Khamenei's authority in Iran – Altahreernews)
 &nbsp;

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:03.060
No marja resigns during his lifetime. And&nbsp;
on top of the fact that, him being an Iraqi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:06.900
ayatollah, and recognizing the religious&nbsp;
authority of Khamenei, Iran's supreme leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:10.860
indicates that Tehran influenced his decision&nbsp;
with the aim of reducing al-Sadr's power.

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:14.400
But this move to subdue the Sadrist Movement's&nbsp;
supporters has failed. His followers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:18.240
remained loyal. And Iraqi nationalism, which&nbsp;
puts political interests before religious ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:22.260
has been strengthened. Clearly Muqtada al-Sadr&nbsp;
is not the perfect ally of the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.740
but given what has been seen in Iraq,&nbsp;
he may be the least bad option.
 &nbsp;

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:29.880
A strong Iraq governed by a coalition of&nbsp;
Shiites, Sunnis and Kurds to oppose Iranian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:33.420
